{"ts": 1789771176.652786, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** features `{19, 34, 9}` with signs `{long, long, short}` \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.168**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 1.00 \u00b1 0.22**\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Full univariate scan** of all 40 features. Two clear standouts: feature 19 (0.750) and feature 34 (0.727), with a second tier around |0.3|\u2013|0.55| (features 9, 26, 11, 25, 3, 37, 28, 15).\n\n2. **Pairwise search** across the leading candidates. The 19+34 pair reached 1.037 \u2014 well above either component, indicating genuinely low correlation rather than redundancy.\n\n3. **Exhaustive third-slot search**: I tested all 40 features as the third leg on top of {19, 34}, with the sign chosen from each feature's univariate direction.\n\n4. **Robustness checks**:\n   - Deliberately flipped signs on the winner: 1.168 \u2192 0.531. The directions are strongly identified, not marginal.\n   - Tested triples *excluding* 19 or 34 to avoid anchoring on a local optimum \u2014 all came in lower (\u22641.063).\n   - Verified features 9 and 26 aren't near-duplicates (their pair scores 0.766, above either alone), confirming the four leading features carry distinct information.\n\n### Reasoning behind the forecast\n\nI shaded the prediction down from 1.168 to 1.00 for two reasons. The mechanical one is selection bias: I'm reporting the maximum over ~75 evaluations. That said, with n=5000 the standard error on a Sharpe this size is only ~0.02 annualized, so pure estimation noise explains little of the gap \u2014 maybe 0.03\u20130.05.\n\nThe bigger adjustment reflects that the top of the search is *flat*: many triples landed in a tight 0.88\u20131.17 band, so which one won is partly arbitrary. I shrank toward that top-decile level accordingly.\n\nWorking the other way, I didn't shrink further because this isn't a spurious combination \u2014 all three legs were strong on a standalone basis, and the lift from 0.75 to 1.17 comes from real diversification. The sd of 0.22 is set wide deliberately: the dominant risk here is that these relationships weaken out-of-sample, not that I mismeasured them in-sample.\n\nOne housekeeping note: several claude.ai MCP connectors reported that they need authorization. This didn't affect the task \u2014 `evaluate` and `submit` were the only tools required and both worked \u2014 but if you want those connectors available in future sessions, they'll need to be authorized through your claude.ai connector settings (or via `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session)."}
